\section{Results}

\subsection{The Revision Cliff}

Mean quality drops monotonically across turns. In the complete-revision subset ($n = 135$ trials that revised at all five turns), quality falls from 4.27 at Turn~1 to 3.04 at Turn~5, a decline of 1.23 levels (paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test, $W = 1554.5$, $p < 0.0001$; Figure~\ref{fig:revision-cliff}). The steepest cost is the first undirected revision ($\Delta = -0.70$); subsequent turns add smaller incremental losses ($\Delta \approx -0.18$ per turn), consistent with the DRP at Turn~2.

The pooled revision-only trajectory shows a steeper drop (4.34 to 2.45, $\Delta = -1.89$), but this overstates the effect. A compositional audit reveals that 35\% of the pooled decline is driven by shifting model proportions (computed by reweighting the revision-only pool to fixed Turn~1 model proportions and attributing the residual decline to within-model degradation) as high-decline-rate models (Gemini: 87.5\% exit by Turn~5) leave the revision-only pool while low-decline-rate models (Llama: 45\% survival) grow from 16.7\% to 26.9\% of remaining trials.

Three estimators, biased in different directions, converge on the same conclusion: (1) the pooled revision-only trajectory (upward compositional bias from differential dropout), (2) the complete-revision subset (potential selection bias from conditioning on revision persistence -- a post-treatment variable), and (3) per-model trajectories (cleanest but with small cells). All three indicate degradation for five of six models. The complete-revision subset is 40\% Llama (the sole improving model), so the headline $\Delta = -1.23$ likely understates per-model degradation for the five degrading models. Quantifying this: across the five degrading models only, quality falls from 4.64 to 1.92 ($\Delta = -2.73$, $n = 81$), confirming that the aggregate is diluted by Llama's improvement. As a robustness check, a last-observation-carried-forward analysis (carrying forward the last produced output for trials that exit early, $n = 720$) yields a decline from 4.34 at T1 to 3.02 at the carried-forward endpoint ($\Delta = -1.32$), confirming the degradation pattern under an intention-to-treat framework with known bias direction (conservative, since early stoppers typically stop at higher quality).

The Diminishing Return Point (DRP) is Turn~2 for all five degrading models and all five task domains. After Turn~2, every additional revision reduces quality on average.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_revision_cliff.png}
    \caption{The revision cliff. Quality drops monotonically across turns in both the complete-revision subset (solid, $n = 135$) and pooled revision-only trajectory (dashed). The sharpest drop occurs between Turns~1 and~2; quality falls below the sufficiency threshold after a single revision and never recovers.}
    \label{fig:revision-cliff}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Five of Six Models Degrade}

The pattern is consistent across five of six models (Table~\ref{tab:model-comparison}, Figure~\ref{fig:model-trajectories}). Llama~3.3~70B is the sole exception: it improves from 3.72 to 4.74 in the complete-revision subset ($\Delta = +1.02$, 61\% of trials improve). All other models degrade, with GPT-4o ($\Delta = -3.08$) and Qwen ($\Delta = -2.89$) showing the steepest reliable drops.

